The task's title promised that /map would honour the /cd deny list. It doesn't, and the commit says so openly:

• **Where the check landed:** `commands_map.rs` has one production `check_path` site, in `build_repo_map_core`. The startup-fence check there is unchanged, and the `/cd` deny list is now checked right after it. Files refused by each list are counted separately, and the /cd ones get their own warning ending in CD_DENY_SOURCE_NOTE.
• **Why `/map itself is untouched:** the /map command doesn't apply the launch fence either, by design (#1002), because its output goes to the user, not the model. Applying only the /cd list there would make /cd stricter than --deny-dir`. The commit gives this reasoning and changes the title to match.
• **No visible change today:** the only caller that applies the fence is the prompt map built at startup, before any `/cd`, so the `/cd` list is always empty when it runs. The new check only matters if that map is rebuilt later.
• **Directory checked:** the map reads the current working directory, which `/cd` changes, so the check guards the right directory. The task's "stop if the base is wrong" condition didn't trigger.
• Register: one row removed from UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS, leaving 4 (commands_project.rs, commands_rename.rs, context.rs, skill_fence.rs). The commit body states 5 → 4 and names them, and the ARCHITECTURE.md list matches.
• **Tests:** they use a temp dir and a passed-in /cd list, never the global one:
  • a /cd entry leaves out private/ while the sibling directory renders exactly as before;
  • an empty or unrelated /cd list gives byte-identical output, with and without a startup fence;
  • when both lists name the same directory, the omission is counted against the startup fence.
